    /// <summary>
    /// The top of the screen of the game. On a screen with the room it is a band of five rows: the name
    /// in letters of blocks, in the colours of the wordmark from Maester red to amber; the hearts as
    /// sprites, full and empty; the score in large digits, with the streak and the best score; and at the
    /// right the run itself. On a smaller screen, and on one without Unicode, it is one line.
    /// </summary>
        // The letters of the name, five rows of blocks each.
== MtConsoleRenderer.Panels.cs
    /// <summary>
    /// The panels of the dashboard: a column to the right of the main content on wide consoles, and the
    /// results chart under the lanes. The caller chooses the panels and their order with SetPanels.
    ///
    /// Text panels (Tenant, Blog, Version, or any other name) show the lines the caller gives them. The live
    /// panels are worked out here from the results as they arrive: Failed (by severity), Pace (and the slowest
    /// tests), Drift (against the results of an earlier run), Tips (one at a time) and Results (one square per
    /// test, in the main column). The insight panels, which count the results by the attributes of the tests,
    /// are in MtConsoleRenderer.Insights.cs.
    /// </summary>
        // As many of the slowest tests as the tallest column can list.
== MtConsoleRenderer.Sections.cs
    /// <summary>
    /// The boxed layout of the main column, on a console that has the rows for it: the phases on a track, and
    /// every other part of the main column a box like the panels of the right column, with its name in the top
    /// border and a colour for what it holds.
    ///
    ///   Track     the phases as stops on a line (BuildTrack). The line after the phase of the tests fills as
    ///             tests finish; the line after another phase that works has a dot that runs along it. Under
    ///             the stops, their times, how far the tests are or what the phase is doing, and the counts.
    ///   Products  one lane per product, in a green box.
    ///   Pace      the graph of how long each test took, when the band at the top has no room for it.
    ///   Tests     one block per test.
    ///   Running   one line per running test, and under a dashed line the tests that finished, newest first,
    ///             in a teal box. It takes the rows down to the insight panels, or to the bottom.
    ///
    /// The boxes cost their borders: about four rows more than the plain lines, which come out of the list
    /// of finished tests. A console with fewer rows, or a narrower one, keeps the plain lines.
    ///
    /// The dashboard moves where work moves, on the tick: a pulse runs along the bar of each product that has
    /// tests running and along the finished part of the track, a dot runs along the line of a phase that works,
    /// and a block that just got its result is bright for a moment. A frame changes a few cells for it, which
    /// the frame of the tick writes as lines.
    /// </summary>
== MtConsoleRenderer.Insights.cs
    /// <summary>
    /// What the dashboard needs to know of a test for its insight panels: the attributes of [MaesterTest] that
    /// the panels count by. Given to the renderer once, before the tests start (SetTestInfo).
    /// </summary>
        /// <summary>The ID the test reports with (the instance ID for a test of a family).</summary>
        /// <summary>Key of the area in the taxonomy registry.</summary>
        /// <summary>MITRE ATT&amp;CK technique IDs.</summary>
        /// <summary>Control references, such as nist-800-53-r5:AC-2.</summary>
== MtConsoleRenderer.Wall.cs
    /// <summary>
    /// How the game comes on screen the first time in a run: the dashboard is a wall that is broken
    /// through. Cracks spread from a point for a moment, then a ragged hole grows from that point until
    /// the dashboard is gone, and the game is what shows through the hole all the while. After that the
    /// card of the help is on it, and the game waits for a key to start.
    ///
    /// The wall is the frame of the dashboard that was on screen, cell by cell with its colours and
    /// without its hyperlinks. A frame of the breaking is put together from it and from the frame of the
    /// game: a cell shows the game once the hole has reached it. Nothing here keeps a state between
    /// frames but the time, and what looks like chance comes from the seed of the wall: the same seed
    /// breaks the same wall the same way.
    ///
    /// The game does not move while the wall breaks, and any key ends the breaking at once. Whatever goes
    /// wrong ends it as well: the game is shown as it is without it.
    /// </summary>
== MtConsoleRenderer.Totals.cs
    /// <summary>
    /// A contributor for the Featured contributor panel of the dashboard: the name, the GitHub handle, how many
    /// tests the person wrote and how many they improved, and the year of their first contribution.
    /// </summary>
    /// <summary>
    /// Two panels of the right column: Totals, the results so far as a bar split by result, and Contributor,
    /// one of the people who built Maester, a different one every minute.
    /// </summary>
== MtConsoleRenderer.Input.cs
    /// <summary>
    /// The keyboard of the dashboard. With ReadKeys set, the tick of the timer takes the keys that were typed
    /// since the last one and hands them to HandleKey; SendKey hands over a key the same way, for tests.
    ///
    /// Keys are read only while the full-screen dashboard runs tests (or shows the game), from a console
    /// that is not redirected, and never by a renderer that writes somewhere else than the console. What is
    /// typed in that time is taken: it does not reach the prompt after the run. Ctrl+C is not a key here
    /// and stops the run as before. A console that cannot be read turns the reading off for the rest of
    /// the run: it must never fail the run or hold it up.
    ///
    /// The reading is done outside the lock of the renderer, by one thread at a time: a read that another
    /// reader of the console holds up then waits alone, and the frames go on.
    /// </summary>
== MtConsoleFeeds.cs
    /// <summary>A post of the blog feed: the day it was published ("MMM dd", or empty), its title and its address.</summary>
    /// <summary>
    /// Fills the Blog panel of the dashboard and looks for a newer version, from the web, on background threads, so a run never
    /// waits for them. Each makes one GET request with a five-second timeout; a failure (offline, proxy, a
    /// changed feed) leaves the panel out. The blog posts are kept in a cache file for a day.
    ///
    /// Invoke-Maester starts them only for a dashboard that is wide enough to show the panels, and never with
    /// -SkipVersionCheck.
    /// </summary>

